Let me look at the fio_keywords table and understand the bug. The issue is in `fio_keyword_replace`:

```c
opt += strlen(kw->word) + olen;
/* keeps final zero thanks to calloc */
if (strlen(opt))
        memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1);
```

The `memcpy` copies `opt - o_org - 1` bytes. But `opt` was advanced by `strlen(kw->word) + olen`, and `o_org` is the original start. So `opt - o_org - 1 = strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`. But the actual remaining string after the keyword is `strlen(opt)` bytes. If `strlen(opt)` is larger than `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`, then the memcpy reads beyond the buffer (heap-buffer-overflow read).

Wait, let me think again. `opt` points to the position after the keyword. `opt - o_org - 1` = `(strlen(kw->word) + olen) - 1`. The remaining string length is `strlen(opt)`. If the remaining string is longer than `strlen(kw->word) + olen - 1`, then memcpy reads `opt - o_org - 1` bytes from `opt`, which is fine as long as `opt - o_org - 1 <= strlen(opt)`. Actually memcpy reads `opt - o_org - 1` bytes starting at `opt`. That's within bounds if `opt - o_org - 1 <= strlen(opt)`. 

